\begin{align*}
x + y + z &= 4, \\
x^2 + y^2 + z^2 &= 6.
\end{align*}
By Cauchy-Schwarz, we have
\[(1^2 + 1^2 + 1^2)(x^2 + y^2 + z^2) \ge (x + y + z)^2,\]or $3 \cdot 6 \ge 16,$ which gives us $18 \ge 16.$
Since the equality condition holds, we can say that $x = y = z.$
Substituting into the equation $x + y + z = 4,$ we get $3x = 4,$ so $x = \frac{4}{3}.$
